- op: create  # b555
  at: '2026-08-13T04:49:02Z'  # b555
  by: agent  # b555
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b555
  clock: 1  # b555
  nonce: b555  # b555
  data:  # b555
    kind: invariant  # b555
    title: "A repo-supplied sanitizer that DELETES a violation is not attributed at the point of deletion, and rc 0 is identical to an earned clean verdict"  # b555
    status: violated  # b555
    priority: 1  # b555
    parent:  # b555
    tags:  # b555
    - security  # b555
    - taint  # b555
    - verify_claims  # b555
    isbefore: []  # b555
    duplicate_of: []  # b555
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b555
    pr_ref:  # b555
    description: "## What is wrong\n\nA verdict says a sanitizer protected the flow. It does not say WHICH sanitizer,\nor that the sanitizer came from the analysed repository rather than from the\nshipped catalogue. And the exit code — the only part of the output a CI gate\nreads — is identical either way.\n\nMeasured at dev adfaaeebf2, shipped CLI, baseline established first (full\nevidence on WI-dagib, which this item is split out of):\n\n    fixture: 11 lines, `os` only\n        secret = os.environ[\"API_KEY\"]\n        safe   = launder(secret)          # launder() returns its argument\n        os.remove(safe)\n    claim:  taint_flow host_secret -> host_fs\n\n    baseline                        -> rc 1 VIOLATED  (\"1 unsanitized\n                                       host_secret flow(s) ... environ -> remove\")\n    + 8-line sanitizer file naming\n      h.launder as plaintext->cipher-> rc 0 CONFIRMED (\"No unsanitized\n                                       host_secret data reaches host_fs zone.\n                                       1 flow(s) reach that zone but pass\n                                       through a sanitizer on every route.\")\n\nThe same lie shipped INSIDE the tree as `.hg-sanitizers.yaml` and referenced\nfrom the repo's own claims file `extra_catalogs:` block gives the same rc 0 with\nno flag passed by whoever runs the tool.\n\n## The precise gap (this is NOT \"users can supply catalogues\")\n\nUser catalogues are trusted BY DESIGN (ADR-0016 §27; INV-zosun chose disclosure\nover restriction deliberately, and that ruling is not being reopened). INV-zosun\nalso WORKS here — the run prints:\n\n    NOTE: these verdicts were computed against USER-SUPPLIED catalogue input.\n      taint_sanitizers: fx2/.hg-sanitizers.yaml  [claims-file extra_catalogs]\n\nSo the input is named. What is missing is the link from the input to the EFFECT:\n\n1. **The verdict text cannot distinguish the two.** `sanitized_clause`\n   (verify_claims.py:1836-1841) interpolates the sanitized-flow COUNT and\n   nothing else, so a lying user entry and a legitimate built-in\n   `Fernet.encrypt` produce byte-identical verdict text. Verified by reading the\n   constructor, not inferred from the runs.\n2. **The exit code carries no trace.** rc 0 whether or not a repo-supplied entry\n   is the reason the claim reads clean. A CI gate keys on rc; the NOTE is prose\n   on stdout that no gate reads.\n\nINV-zosun's disclosure was designed for the OVERLAY case, where a row only\nGRANTS coverage. A sanitizer DELETES a finding the tool had already made. That\nis strictly stronger, and it was unmeasured when the disclosure-over-restriction\ncall was made.\n\n## Why this matters beyond a lab fixture\n\nIt is the RCT's trust story. A participant repo that ships its own claims file\nplus a sanitizer entry gets a green verdict, and the artifact a reader keeps —\nthe exit code, or a one-line \"all N CONFIRMED\" — records nothing about it.\n\n## Candidate remedies (none costed; do not pick one from this text alone)\n\na. **Attribute at the point of deletion.** Name the sanitizer(s) that removed\n   the flow in the verdict details, and mark user-supplied ones. Smallest, and\n   strictly an improvement regardless of what else is done.\nb. **Make the exit code carry it** — a distinct rc for \"confirmed, but a\n   repo-supplied entry is load-bearing\". Changes the CI contract; needs care.\nc. **ADR-0016's unimplemented fourth verdict** (\"confirmed with caveats\") —\n   this is its second and stronger consumer (the first was the overlay case).\n   Consumers branch on `verdict`, so it is the largest.\n\n(a) is independent of the ruling on (b)/(c) and does not change any verdict.\n\n## Not claimed\n\n- Not a third-party exploit: it requires the repo owner, or whoever runs the\n  tool, to write the file.\n- NOT SWEPT: I tested sanitizers only. Whether user-supplied SOURCES or SINKS\n  can suppress a finding by the same route is untested, and \"untested\" is not\n  \"safe\" — the same distinction WI-dagib was filed to protect."  # b555
    fields:  # b555
      statement: "When a user-supplied catalogue entry is the reason a claim reads clean, the verdict must say so where the reader and any CI gate will see it: the verdict text must attribute the sanitizer and mark it user-supplied, and the exit code must not be indistinguishable from a verdict the analysis earned on its own."  # b555
      root_cause: "sanitized_clause (verify_claims.py:1836-1841) is built from the sanitized-flow COUNT alone, so a lying user entry and a built-in Fernet.encrypt produce byte-identical text; rc is 0 in both cases. INV-zosun's disclosure names the INPUT but never links it to the EFFECT, and it was designed for the overlay case (which only grants coverage) rather than for deletion (which removes a finding already made)."  # b555
- op: discuss  # 6924
  at: '2026-08-13T04:50:00Z'  # 6924
  by: agent  # 6924
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6924
  clock: 2  # 6924
  nonce: '6924'  # 6924
  message: "COSTING for remedy (a), done before proposing it so the choice is informed.\n\n(a) SPLITS INTO TWO REMEDIES OF VERY DIFFERENT SIZE, because the identity a\nreader wants is not currently carried anywhere.\n\n  TaintFlowFinding.sanitized is a plain bool (taint.py:209ff — fields are\n  taint_label, source_symbol, source_primitive, sink_symbol, sink_primitive,\n  sink_zone, sanitized, confidence, analysis_method, path, source_module,\n  sink_module). Nothing records WHICH sanitizer fired, and the sanitizer\n  registry does not record whether an entry came from a shipped catalogue or a\n  user path. verify_claims only ever sees the bool: sanitized_flows is computed\n  as len(constrained) - len(matching) at verify_claims.py:1790.\n\n  a1 EXACT ATTRIBUTION (\"...on every route (via h.launder, project-local)\").\n     Multi-site: record provenance per sanitizer entry at registration; carry\n     the firing sanitizer onto the finding (new field); render it; bump\n     VERIFY_CLAIMS_SCHEMA_VERSION for the JSON envelope. This is the version\n     that actually answers 'was a repo-supplied entry load-bearing'.\n\n  a2 RUN-LEVEL CAVEAT (\"...on every route; a project-local sanitizer catalogue\n     was in effect for this run\"). Small — both inputs already exist at the\n     verify_claims call site, since INV-zosun assembles catalogue provenance\n     there. But it is WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS and the weakness must be stated: it\n     says a user catalogue was PRESENT, not that it CHANGED anything. A repo\n     whose user catalogue is inert gets the identical sentence, so a reader who\n     learns to discount the caveat learns to discount it everywhere. That is\n     the shape of a disclosure that decays into noise.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: a1, or nothing. a2 buys a sentence that cannot distinguish the\ncase this item was filed about from a harmless one, which is close to what the\ncurrent output already does wrong. If a1 is too big to schedule now, leaving the\ngap OPEN and documented is more honest than shipping a caveat that reads as\nthough the question were answered.\n\n(b) exit-code change and (c) the ADR-0016 fourth verdict both remain uncosted\nand both change a consumer contract; neither should be bundled with (a)."  # 6924
- op: discuss  # 96ac
  at: '2026-08-13T06:53:35Z'  # 96ac
  by: agent  # 96ac
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 96ac
  clock: 3  # 96ac
  nonce: 96ac  # 96ac
  message: "REMEDY (a1) LANDED at dev 1ec23deb31. Item stays VIOLATED: (a1) closes the 'verdict text cannot distinguish' half; the exit-code half is untouched.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE, re-run on the MERGED tree with both controls in the same session:\n\n  1. repo-supplied lie (dotfile inside the tree, referenced from the repo's own\n     claims-file extra_catalogs block):\n       Verdict: confirmed\n       '...pass through a sanitizer on every route (via h.launder (project-local)).'\n     Before: the identical sentence WITHOUT any attribution.\n\n  2. DISCRIMINATOR, shipped sanitizer, no user catalogue:\n       '(barrier matched one of: cryptography.fernet.Fernet.encrypt,\n         ...AESGCM.encrypt, ...AESCCM.encrypt, ...ChaCha20Poly1305.encrypt)'\n     Named, and NOT marked project-local. This is the control that keeps the\n     marking meaningful: a caveat printed on every sanitized verdict would carry\n     no information and a reader would learn to discount it.\n\n  3. CONTROL, same fixture with no sanitizer catalogue at all: rc 1 VIOLATED.\n     The finding is still made; only what the verdict SAYS changed.\n\nWHAT LANDED. sanitized_by / sanitized_by_user_supplied on TaintFlowFinding,\nserialized into the JSON envelope; provenance stamped once where the user layer\nis assembled; attribution wired into BOTH arms (the call-graph barrier exempts\nthe seed function, so the same-function shape -- which is what the repro is --\nis credited only by the DDG 3a arm; wiring one arm left the filed case still\nprinting the unattributed clause).\n\nA DEFECT IN MY OWN FIRST VERSION, worth recording because it is this item's own\nfailure mode turned on the fix: sanitizer_callers kept ONE slot per (caller,\ntaint_label), and all four shipped *.encrypt sanitizers match a bare 'encrypt'\ncallee, so the last registered overwrote the rest and a Fernet.encrypt call was\nattributed to ChaCha20Poly1305.encrypt. The barrier never noticed because it\nonly asks WHETHER some sanitizer carries the label; attribution asks WHICH.\nFound by running the live discriminator AFTER the unit tests were green. Fixed\nby keeping every candidate and letting the wording follow the evidence ('via X'\nonly when there is exactly one, 'barrier matched one of: ...' otherwise), and\npinned by test_several_candidate_sanitizers_are_not_reported_as_one.\n\nSTILL OPEN, and the reason this item is not satisfied:\n  - rc 0 is IDENTICAL whether or not a repo-supplied entry is load-bearing. A\n    CI gate keys on rc; prose is not a gate. That is remedy (b) (distinct exit\n    code) or (c) (ADR-0016's unimplemented fourth verdict). Both change a\n    consumer contract and were deliberately not bundled.\n  - NOT SWEPT: whether user-supplied SOURCES or SINKS can suppress a finding by\n    the same route is still untested (carried over from WI-dagib). Untested is\n    not safe."  # 96ac
